{
    "script": [
        {
            "scene_name": "Chapter 1: Introduction to Computational Humor",
            "scene_music": "ChillLofiR.mp3",
            "interactions": [
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "Hey! Today, we're diving into something fascinating: Computational Humor! Can computers really *get* jokes?",
                    "character_expression": "happy",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "This paper explores how well computers can generate and *explain* humor. It's not as simple as you might think. Humor is abstract, creative, and depends a lot on context.",
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Player",
                    "dialogue": {
                        "type": "conversational",
                        "prompt": "Why is understanding humor important for AI?",
                        "choices": [
                            {
                                "text": "It helps them understand human communication better.",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "It's just a fun challenge for programmers.",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "Computers understanding jokes? What a world we live in...",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "scene_name": "Chapter 2: The Building Blocks: Theories of Humor",
            "scene_music": "ChillLofiR.mp3",
            "interactions": [
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "Before we get too far, let's look at some theories about what *makes* something funny. There's Relief Theory, Superiority Theory... but the big one for AI is Incongruity Theory.",
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "Incongruity means something is unexpected or out of place. A joke's punchline often creates an incongruity that makes us laugh!",
                    "character_expression": "happy",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Player",
                    "dialogue": {
                        "type": "re-explanation",
                        "prompt": "So, humor is about things not making sense? Could you give me an example?",
                        "choices": [
                            {
                                "text": "Yes, please give an example",
                                "outcome": "replay"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "No, I think I get it.",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "Sure! Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything! The incongruity is that 'make up' can mean 'invent' or 'compose.'",
                    "character_expression": "happy",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "scene_name": "Chapter 3: Generating Puns: A Playground for AI",
            "scene_music": "ChillLofiR.mp3",
            "interactions": [
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "Alright, let's talk about how computers *make* jokes. A big focus has been on puns, because they're relatively straightforward.",
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "There are two main types: homographic puns, which use words with multiple meanings, and heterographic puns, which rely on similar sounds.",
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Player",
                    "dialogue": {
                        "type": "question",
                        "prompt": "What's the difference between homographic and heterographic puns?",
                        "choices": [
                            {
                                "text": "Homographic puns use similar meanings, heterographic use similar sounds.",
                                "outcome": "wrong"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "Homographic puns use multiple meanings, heterographic use similar sounds.",
                                "outcome": "correct"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "Heterographic puns use multiple meanings, homographic use similar sounds.",
                                "outcome": "wrong"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "Exactly! For example, a homographic pun: 'Time flies like an arrow. Fruit flies like a banana.' 'Flies' has two meanings. A heterographic pun? Less common since it's harder.",
                    "character_expression": "happy",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "scene_name": "Chapter 4: Explaining the Joke: The Real Challenge",
            "scene_music": "ChillLofiR.mp3",
            "interactions": [
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "Generating humor is one thing, but *explaining* why something is funny? That's where things get interesting! One famous quote says that if a computer can explain a joke, it really does understand.",
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "The paper looks at two approaches: classifying the type of humor and providing a natural language explanation.",
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Player",
                    "dialogue": {
                        "type": "conversational",
                        "prompt": "Which approach do you think is more valuable?",
                        "choices": [
                            {
                                "text": "Classification",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "Natural Language Explanation",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "Both",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "The paper says that while classification has its place, natural language explanations provide deeper insights, especially for complex jokes.",
                    "character_expression": "happy",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "scene_name": "Chapter 5: The Future of Computational Humor",
            "scene_music": "ChillLofiR.mp3",
            "interactions": [
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "So, where is this all heading? The paper points to some exciting directions for future research. The first is demographic-aware humor, jokes tailored to specific audiences.",
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "Also, the ability to know someone's intent behind making the joke is important. It can help to avoid the excuse of 'I was just joking' when hate speech has been said.",
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Player",
                    "dialogue": {
                        "type": "re-explanation",
                        "prompt": "Intent-aware humor... why is that important?",
                        "choices": [
                            {
                                "text": "Explain it again, please.",
                                "outcome": "replay"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "I think I understand.",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "Well, intent can help determine if the reference is acceptable, or if it is being used to spread hate. The 'it's just a joke' defense has been a harmful idea, and AI may be able to help see past that.",
                    "character_expression": "happy",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Player",
                    "dialogue": {
                        "type": "question",
                        "prompt": "What's one ethical consideration of computational humor mentioned in the paper?",
                        "choices": [
                            {
                                "text": "Making jokes that are too intellectual.",
                                "outcome": "wrong"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "Unintentionally spreading harmful biases or stereotypes.",
                                "outcome": "correct"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "Using too much computing power.",
                                "outcome": "wrong"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "scene_name": "Chapter 6: Conclusion",
            "scene_music": "ChillLofiR.mp3",
            "interactions": [
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "So, there you have it! Computational humor is an unsolved task with real-world applications. It's not just about making computers funny; it's about understanding language, context, and even ethics.",
                    "character_expression": "happy",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Kurisu",
                    "dialogue": "Future work needs to consider a broader range of humor and give explicit attention to the ethics involved. It's a fascinating field with a lot of potential!",
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                },
                {
                    "character_name": "Player",
                    "dialogue": {
                        "type": "conversational",
                        "prompt": "What was your favorite thing you learned?",
                        "choices": [
                            {
                                "text": "That AI may be able to see when jokes are used to spread hate.",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "The difference between puns!",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            },
                            {
                                "text": "More about how humor works.",
                                "outcome": "next"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    "character_expression": "neutral",
                    "image_displayed": "image.png"
                }
            ]
        }
    ]
}